<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
    Resources/Vigil-Dev.entitlements — local development only. NEVER DISTRIBUTED.

    Implements docs/ARCHITECTURE.md §13.4. Selected by the sandbox-off flag of build-app.sh, which
    prints a loud warning and stamps VigilDevBuild=true into the bundle's Info.plist so the About
    window can show it.

    Why it exists: turning the sandbox off removes the provisioning-profile requirement around the
    managed multicast entitlement, which is what unblocks day-to-day SADP work on a laptop that has
    no Apple grant.

    What it does NOT do: unsandboxed is still subject to the macOS 15 local-network prompt, so a
    denied prompt still makes discovery silently find nothing.

    com.apple.security.get-task-allow permits a debugger to attach. It must be absent from anything
    that leaves the build machine; a notarisation submission carrying it is rejected.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>

    <key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
    <false/>

    <key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
    <true/>

    <key>com.apple.security.network.server</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- The default clip and snapshot destination, ~/Movies/Vigil.
         Without this, `.moviesDirectory` resolves to `Data/Movies` inside the container, which is a
         symlink to the real folder — so `createDirectory` fails with NSCocoaErrorDomain 513
         (NSFileWriteNoPermissionError) and recording refuses to start. Observed on the first real
         recording attempt; the path in the error is what named the cause.
         `files.user-selected.read-write` below does NOT cover this: it grants only what the user
         picks in a panel, and the default destination is never picked. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.assets.movies.read-write</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- Snapshots land in ~/Pictures/Vigil, the way clips land in ~/Movies/Vigil, and
         they need their own grant: the two asset entitlements are separate keys. Without
         this the sandbox refuses the write with NSCocoaErrorDomain 513 at a path that
         looks perfectly ordinary — which is exactly how the movies grant was found
         missing, after Record appeared to work and produced no file. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.assets.pictures.read-write</key>
    <true/>

    <key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- Debugger attach. Development only. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.get-task-allow</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- ⚠️ Do NOT add a keychain-access-groups entitlement here. It looks like the fix for the
         "Vigil is not allowed to use the keychain" card on the first connect: CredentialStore asks
         for the data-protection keychain, that keychain needs a keychain access group, and an
         ad-hoc signature (no CODESIGN_IDENTITY, hence no Team ID) has none, so every SecItem call
         returns errSecMissingEntitlement. But keychain-access-groups is a restricted entitlement:
         AMFI refuses to launch an ad-hoc-signed process that carries one without a matching
         provisioning profile, and the app dies at spawn with "Launchd job spawn failed" (POSIX 163)
         before any code runs. That is strictly worse than the keychain card. The dev build's
         keychain problem is solved in code instead: CredentialStore falls back to the legacy file
         keychain, which needs no access group, when VigilDevBuild is stamped into Info.plist. See
         Sources/VigilCore/Security/CredentialStore.swift and Sources/Vigil/AppSessionModel.swift. -->

</dict>
</plist>
